11
00:00:51,160 --> 00:00:56,780
 So like I mentioned in the very first video, Peridontal disease is an interplay between

12
00:00:56,780 --> 00:00:59,760
 bacteria and the host.

13
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:05,720
 Plaque is the initiating factor, and the host responds often damaging its own tissue in

14
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:07,880
 order to protect itself.

15
00:01:07,880 --> 00:01:12,860
 So it's important to talk about the immune cells that play a part in this response.

16
00:01:12,860 --> 00:01:17,920
 So the first cell we'll talk about is the neutrophil, and neutrophils are the first

17
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:23,080
 line of defense, and they're considered the most important cells involved in controlling

18
00:01:23,080 --> 00:01:30,880
 this bacterial challenge and destroying peridontal tissue via release of destructive molecules.

19
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:36,880
 So all of those bacteria that we talked about in the third video, like P. gingivalis and

20
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:42,720
 all of these other pathogenic bacteria, are the attackers, neutrophils are the primary

21
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:44,720
 defenders.

22
00:01:44,720 --> 00:01:51,440
 So neutrophils are going to migrate from blood vessels that are underneath the gingiva into

23
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:59,200
 the peridontal pocket by directed locomotion known as chemotaxis, where they form a barrier

24
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:05,120
 to protect the body from these peridontal pathogenic bacteria.

25
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:10,760
 They internalize the bacteria via phagocytosis, so they eat them and then kill them using

26
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:12,960
 this biologic bleach.

27
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:18,960
 It's a really nasty chemical of myeloparoxide and oxygen radicals.

28
00:02:18,960 --> 00:02:24,440
 So this stuff is nasty for the bacteria and they can't really survive with this mix of

29
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:25,440
 chemicals.

30
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:32,880
 MMP8, also known as neutrophil collagenase, is the most important proteinase involved

31
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:40,920
 in destruction of peridontal tissues, and it's inhibited by this antibiotic tetracycline.

32
00:02:40,920 --> 00:02:47,160
 So MMPs are really important destructive enzymes to know, and they'll come up again later in

33
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:53,380
 the video, and basically what this slide is saying is that neutrophils are the first responders,

34
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:59,480
 they sniff out the invading bacteria, follow where they are by chemotaxis, eat them by

35
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:08,880
 phagocytosis, and then release strong destructive enzymes that not only kill the bacteria inside

36
00:03:08,880 --> 00:03:15,240
 of them, but they also destroy peridontal tissue around them.

37
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:23,760
 So neutrophils, like any cell in the body, can perform not well, and there can be abnormalities

38
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:25,480
 associated with them.

39
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:32,360
 So defective neutrophil chemotaxis, for example, can lead to aggressive peridontitis.

40
00:03:32,360 --> 00:03:36,760
 So we talked about certain bacteria that can cause aggressive peridontitis, that would

41
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:45,200
 be the really long name, acrogatibacter, actinomycetimcomatans, we remember that because the AA bacteria

42
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:53,920
 causes the A form of peridontitis, but also a problem with the own body response can cause

43
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:58,800
 this form of peridontitis as well, so that's important to know.

44
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,040
 The problem with neutrophils and when they don't work properly is it's a lose-lose situation.

45
00:04:04,040 --> 00:04:09,440
 If you have too much neutrophil activity, you can cause too much tissue destruction, and

46
00:04:09,440 --> 00:04:15,520
 if you have too little, it can lead to an unchecked bacteria challenge, which also leads

47
00:04:15,520 --> 00:04:17,280
 to tissue destruction.

48
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:24,880
 So it has to be just right in order to prevent this unnecessary tissue damage, but of course,

49
00:04:24,880 --> 00:04:31,120
 the best possible scenario is to not have these invading pathogenic bacteria.

50
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:36,760
 And other neutrophil abnormalities include these, I wouldn't need to know those for

51
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:42,120
 the board exam, so don't worry so much about those.

52
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:47,280
 So the next immune cell we'll talk about is the macrophage, and these are antigen-presenting

53
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:54,640
 cells, including monocytes and dendritic cells, and they regulate the immune response by releasing

54
00:04:54,640 --> 00:04:58,840
 cytokines like IL-8.

55
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:01,000
 Mass cells are also involved.

56
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:09,960
 They cause vascular permeability and dilation of blood vessels, and they're really well-known

57
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:12,040
 to produce IgE.

58
00:05:12,040 --> 00:05:19,080
 This is immunoglobulin E, and they're antibodies that are famously produced by mass cells.

59
00:05:19,080 --> 00:05:26,480
 This is a pretty common board question, part one and part two boards could ask this sort

60
00:05:26,480 --> 00:05:27,480
 of question.

61
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:32,720
 Remembering IgE is always associated with the mass cell.

62
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:39,520
 And lymphocytes is another category of immune cells to know for the peridontal response.

63
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:44,840
 So B cells become plasma cells and make antibodies.

64
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:52,760
 The T helper cells, also known as CD4 cells, that's because the CD4 is a glycoprotein found

65
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:58,920
 on the surface of these cells, and they help with communication between all the different

66
00:05:58,920 --> 00:06:00,560
 immune cells.

67
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:07,400
 The T set of toxic cells, also called CD8 cells, because CD8 is a glycoprotein found

68
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:14,960
 on the surface of these cells, they directly kill intracellular antigens.

69
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:19,720
 And then we have NK cells, which stands for natural killer cells.

70
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:24,340
 There are specific type of T cell that can recognize and kill tumor and virally infected

71
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:25,780
 cells.

72
00:06:25,780 --> 00:06:32,000
 So all of these cells can, of course, like the macrophages and the mass cells and the

73
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,520
 neutrophils.

74
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:38,960
 There's a lot of interplay in the immune system, and so each of these has a very specific role

75
00:06:38,960 --> 00:06:45,400
 to play when responding to any sort of infection.

76
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:53,020
 So the pro-inflammatory mediators are listed here, these are those chemicals that favor

77
00:06:53,020 --> 00:06:58,360
 the destructive nature of peridontal disease, where the body destroys itself in order to

78
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:01,760
 protect itself from the peridontal pathogens.

79
00:07:01,760 --> 00:07:08,800
 That's that sort of innate irony of peridontal disease, or the oxymoron of peridontal disease.

