00:08:30,360 --> 00:08:33,960
 seesaw.

96
00:08:33,960 --> 00:08:41,680
 So some specifics, the pathogenesis of gingivitis, there are four different stages outlined here.

97
00:08:41,680 --> 00:08:47,800
 The initial lesion occurs within two to four days, and it starts with neutrophil infiltration.

98
00:08:47,800 --> 00:08:52,740
 Again, that's that first line of defense by the human body.

99
00:08:52,740 --> 00:08:59,180
 Stage two is considered an early lesion, that's from four to seven days, and the T lymphocytes

100
00:08:59,180 --> 00:09:04,940
 are the next cell to sort of step up to the plate and attack.

101
00:09:04,940 --> 00:09:09,880
 When you see the early lesion, you see increased collagen loss and bleeding on probing.

102
00:09:09,880 --> 00:09:15,040
 So more classic signs of gingival inflammation.

103
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:17,880
 Stage three is considered an established lesion.

104
00:09:17,880 --> 00:09:23,200
 This is 14 to 21 days, and then the B cells step up to the plate.

105
00:09:23,200 --> 00:09:29,720
 They infiltrate, including mature, they also grow into mature plasma cells, so they're

106
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:36,160
 releasing antibodies, and causing more collagen loss, clinical changes in color, contour,

107
00:09:36,160 --> 00:09:37,160
 and consistency.

108
00:09:37,160 --> 00:09:42,080
 Again, those are our three C's associated with gingivitis, and now we're really getting

109
00:09:42,080 --> 00:09:45,840
 into an established gingivitis situation.

110
00:09:45,840 --> 00:09:51,120
 And stage four, an advanced lesion, this is where we get transitioned to irreversible

111
00:09:51,120 --> 00:09:52,120
 damage.

112
00:09:52,120 --> 00:09:58,080
 Again, all this, these first three stages are the reversible stages of gingivitis.

113
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:04,600
 Once we get bone loss at stage four, that irreversible damage, now we've transitioned

114
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:10,800
 to periodontitis, where we have irreversible tissue damage.

115
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:16,880
 So as mentioned before, environmental and systemic factors like smoking and diabetes

116
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:22,880
 might influence the progression of periodontal disease, but these things don't cause disease.

117
00:10:22,880 --> 00:10:28,960
 Again, without plaque, you're not going to get this immune response, you're not going

118
